.TH CPUMON 1 "2026-09-18" "cpumon 1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
cpumon \- scrollable, man-page-styled Linux CPU usage monitor
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B cpumon
[\fIOPTIONS\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B cpumon
reads
.I /proc/stat
(aggregate and per-core user/nice/system/idle/iowait/irq/softirq/steal
jiffie counters, context switches, forks, running/blocked process counts)
along with
.IR /proc/loadavg ,
.IR /proc/uptime "  and  " /proc/cpuinfo ,
and displays it either as a scrollable interactive terminal display that
auto-refreshes on a timer, or logs it to a daily file in headless
.B \-\-daemon
mode, intended to run under systemd.
.PP
CPU usage is a rate, not a snapshot: every measurement is the delta between
two
.I /proc/stat
reads. The first frame of any mode is produced from a 200ms bootstrap
sample pair; subsequent frames use the delta between consecutive interval
ticks.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-i ", " \-\-interval " " \fIINTERVAL\fR
Refresh/log interval. Accepts plain seconds or a suffix: \fBs\fR (seconds),
\fBm\fR (minutes), \fBh\fR (hours), \fBd\fR (days). Examples: \fB5\fR,
\fB5s\fR, \fB5m\fR, \fB1h\fR. Default: \fB5s\fR.
.TP
.BR \-d ", " \-\-daemon
Headless mode: no interactive display, just logs a snapshot on each
interval. Intended for systemd. Handles SIGTERM/SIGINT for clean shutdown.
.TP
.BR \-o ", " \-\-once
Print a single snapshot to stdout and exit. No interactive display, no
loop, no log file written.
.TP
.BR \-p ", " \-\-plain
Interactive watch mode without ncurses: plain ANSI clear-screen/redraw
instead of full cursor addressing. Use this if the default ncurses
display looks garbled or fails to redraw in place on your terminal
(seen on some Windows/WSL terminal front-ends).
.TP
.BR \-l ", " \-\-log\-dir " " \fIDIR\fR
Directory for daily log files, named \fIYYYY\-MM\-DD.log\fR. Default:
\fI/var/log/cpumon\fR (falls back to \fI~/.local/share/cpumon\fR if that
is not writable).
.TP
.BR \-r ", " \-\-log\-retention\-days " " \fIN\fR
Delete log files older than \fIN\fR days automatically. \fBN=0\fR disables
auto-deletion and keeps logs forever. Default: \fB30\fR. Can also be set via
the \fBCPUMON_LOG_RETENTION_DAYS\fR environment variable (the systemd unit
sets this from \fI/etc/cpumon/cpumon.conf\fR); an explicit \fB\-r\fR on the
command line takes precedence.
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-no\-log
Disable logging while in interactive mode.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show usage help and exit.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-version
Show version and exit.
.SH INTERACTIVE KEYS
.TP
.B q
Quit.
.TP
.B "up/down or j/k"
Scroll one line.
.TP
.B "PgUp/PgDn"
Scroll one page.
.TP
.B "g / G"
Jump to top / bottom of the report.
.TP
.B r
Refresh immediately and reset the countdown.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /var/log/cpumon/YYYY\-MM\-DD.log
Default daily log location (see \fB\-\-log\-dir\fR). Files older than the
retention window (see \fB\-\-log\-retention\-days\fR) are deleted automatically.
.TP
.I /etc/cpumon/cpumon.conf
Configuration sourced by the systemd unit (interval, log directory, log
retention). Edit and run \fBsystemctl restart cpumon\fR to apply.
.TP
.I /lib/systemd/system/cpumon.service
systemd unit for running cpumon as a headless logging daemon.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
cpumon
Interactive display, refreshing every 5 seconds.
.TP
cpumon \-i 5m
Interactive display, refreshing every 5 minutes.
.TP
cpumon \-\-once
One-shot plain text report.
.TP
cpumon \-\-daemon \-i 5m
Headless logger, as used by the systemd service.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR top (1),
.BR mpstat (1),
.BR proc (5)
